  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a3/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_7.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Vulture escapes from prison after fashioning a new flying device that he uses to fly over the prison grounds wall.&nbsp; At school, Peter Parker hears about it and feigns sickness so he can go home and change to Spider-Man to be ready for him.&nbsp; The Vulture, now refit with proper outfit, robs a jewelry showroom before running into Spider-Man, who thinks the anti-magnetic inverter will work once again, so the Vulture allows him to get close enough to use the device and pretends that he's spiraling to the ground, only to turn and strike Spider-Man as he reaches the edge of a rooftop.\n\nA short struggle ensues, and Spider-Man falls to a rooftop, seemingly defeated, causing the Vulture to gloat and fly off.&nbsp; But Peter wakes up and finds his arm badly sprained from the impact.&nbsp; Aunt May takes him to the doctor, who says his arm \"will be fine in a few weeks!\"&nbsp; Peter takes a lot of ribbing at school of course, while the Vulture hides out in an abandoned farm silo on Staten Island.&nbsp; His next heist will be the Daily Bugle, which has \"one of the biggest payrolls in New York.\"&nbsp; He's after the \"hundreds of nice, fat, juicy pay envelopes.\"&nbsp; (<i>Whoa, they got paid in <b>cash</b>?&nbsp; Could be - this was 1963 after all...</i>)\n\nPeter goes to the Bugle to sell Jonah his latest photo, only to have the Vulture show up in person and demand the entire payroll.&nbsp; Jameson is so tight with money, he actually argues with the Vulture over it while being held at gunpoint!&nbsp; Peter slips away and manages to change to Spider-Man and web up his bad arm.&nbsp; The two engage in battle right in the middle of the Daily Bugle offices, with Jameson hurling out threats and pleading for them to stop before they wreck the place!\n\nThey descend into the press room and then out of the building, where the Vulture hauls Spider-Man way up into the air, preparing to drop him before Spider-Man webs up his wings, jamming them up, and the pair begin to fall, but Spider-Man whips up a web parachute and then, once low enough to reach the buildings, leaves the Vulture alone to descend into the hands of the police.&nbsp; (This reminds me of the cliff-hanger ending of <i>Amazing Spider-Man</i> 157 / ASM #157, where Spider-Man is falling down into pasturelands where there are no buildings to grab hold of and cries out \"Face it hero - this time you've bought the farm!\"&nbsp; I guess he completely forgot about this event from years earlier.)\n\nBut as he prepares to swing away, Jameson shouts out how he holds him personally responsible and won't rest until he finds out who he is.&nbsp; Spider-Man webs his mouth shut.&nbsp; \"What have I got to lose?&nbsp; You couldn't hate me any more than you do <i>now</i>!\"&nbsp; He heads back into the Daily Bugle to change back into Peter Parker and finds Betty Brant hiding behind a desk and joins her, claiming he was hiding in a closet.\n\nBetty and Peter share a moment together, with Peter complimenting her perfume, and Betty is thrilled with the romantic undertones, and the two of them snuggle up together.",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME -- write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME -- write something",
  "review_text": "An almost solid tale, not quite rising to being downright memorable.&nbsp; True, we get to see Spider-Man quipping with Jonah Jameson, which is always fun, and lo-and-behold, it's clear Peter and Betty have discovered each other, but the mechanics of the story were largely formulaic, and not quite rising to full mid-grade level.\n\n<i>However</i>, to the issue's credit, the dialogue between Spider-Man and Jameson was excellent, even hilarious at times.&nbsp; That, together with the scene of Jonah arguing with the Vulture at gunpoint - which was <i>classic</i> and so in-character for him - are worth awarding an extra half-point, elevating the story to full mid-grade in the end.&nbsp; The banter was a lot of fun to read.\n\nLet's face it - the Vulture is just <i>not</i> Doc Ock or the Sandman (or the still-to-come and even deadlier foes of Spider-Man such as the Green Goblin or Kingpin, to name just a couple), which make for more interesting foes, but being only the 7<sup>th</sup> issue and having just featured the first two aforementioned villains, they used what they had, and it was noted on the splash page that readers were actually clamoring to see more of the Vulture, so his reuse can be forgiven in that respect.\n\nBut...I <i>have</i> to wonder what Betty is thinking.&nbsp; She's a professional girl, presumably already a high-school graduate, possibly having spent at least one year in some sort of secretarial training to become qualified enough to be the personal assistant of the publisher of a major New York City newspaper (for some perspective on what kind of qualifications this requires, look at all the trouble Jameson later had <i>replacing</i> Brant in issues in the vicinity of 157-159), and yet she's flirting with Peter Parker, who's right-around 16 years old!&nbsp; She's at <i>least</i> a couple of years older than him, probably more.&nbsp; I find it hard to believe that she would not have any other interested guys her own age.&nbsp; The tale suffers from this in my opinion, and even with the previous foreshadowing an issue or two earlier, it still comes across as surprising.\n\nIn its defense however, let's chalk this up to different times - employees received fat, juicy pay envelopes filled with cash at the end of the work week, and apparently, young professional girls flirted with high school guys.&nbsp; I was only a 1 year-old old when this story was published, so who am I to say if this was or wasn't the way things were?&nbsp; It's not fair to judge a story out of the context of its day, so while my 21<sup>st</sup> century eyes might widen at these aspects and cause me to raise questions, I will keep trying for the most part <i>not</i> to cast judgment.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThis issue was an improvement over the last one, bringing back a former villain for the first time, and taking full advantage of the wealth of the supporting cast.&nbsp; The story itself wasn't stupendous, but so many of the other aspects were that it easily rises to mid-grade level overall.",
  "letters_page": "Paul Gambaccini's letter contained some funny comments about Stan's self-promotion:&nbsp; \"And it's not the same old Stan Lee either - whose claims of 'We're superior!&nbsp; We're different!&nbsp; We're the best!&nbsp; We're too great for words!' were trying to promote his own work (sort of like most of your competitors).\"\n\nHowever, what might be the most far-reaching of his comments was what followed:&nbsp; \"I don't know if their stuff has deteriorated or whether you have improved that much, but the competition now seems like [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=bold; text=echh!]\"\n\nEven more amazing was the response:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=italic; text=We appreciate your praise, Paul, but feel we must pause for a serious word to you, and all of fandom.&nbsp; We don't feel it's fair for you to knock any other group of magazines while boosting ours.&nbsp; After all, it's just a matter of taste -- and there are probably many fans who prefer our competitors' mags to ours.&nbsp; There are many fine talents producing well-written and well-drawn stories at other houses besides MARVEL.&nbsp; While we admit we try to create the greatest comics possible, we feel it isn't proper to comment about mags other than our own on these pages.&nbsp; Okay?]\n\nThis serious and respectful response is eye-opening in the light of what later transitions to the open mocking of [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Brand Echh] by none other than Stan himself.&nbsp; However, we have to remember that it's still early in Marvel's existence, and the small staff size and minimal output at the time, together with no established record of long-term success, may well have contributed to this [SPAN: severity=info; text=early, measured, and <i>far</i> more cautionary stance.]",
  "highlight": "FIXME -- write something"
}